_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 4
---
order: 4
---
short_label: VI.4
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: In equiangular triangles the sides about the equal angles are proportional where the corresponding sides are opposite the equal angles.
----
proof:

Let {ABC|ABCt} and {DCE|CDEt} be equiangular triangles having the angle {ABC|angABC} equal to the angle {DCE}, the angle {BAC} equal to the angle {CDE|angCDE}, and the angle {ACB} equal to the angle {CED}.

I say that in the triangles {ABC|ABCt} and {DEC|CDEt} the sides about the equal angles are proportional where the corresponding sides are opposite the equal angles.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="330" height="300" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="VI.4 diagram" src="propVI4.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Euclid sets the triangles base to base (BC in a straight line with
    // CE), produces BA and ED to meet at F, and reads the proportions off
    // the parallelogram FACD and the triangle FBE (VI.2, V.7, V.16, V.22).
    //
    // Joyce's figure draws both triangles (random faces), AF and DF; D1, D2
    // are his hidden parallelogram helpers. The proof also names the sides,
    // the lines FB and FE, the parallelogram FACD, the triangle FBE and six
    // angles. Everything added is transparent (;0;0;0 lines, ;0;0;0;0
    // polygons), so the resting figure is unchanged. The two triangles'
    // own polygons have filled faces, which a highlight would raise over the
    // markers (#140), so caption and prose refs to "the triangles" go to the
    // faceless twins ABCt / CDEt. The polygon CDE owns that name, so the
    // angle CDE takes the display override {CDE|angCDE}; likewise the
    // angle ABC against the triangle ABC. AF and DF are declared A→F and
    // D→F, so their own strokes run outward as "produced": no solid twins.
    //
    // Markers: equal angles share a colour — ABC with DCE (blue), BAC with
    // CDE (purple), ACB with CED (green). The two at C are adjacent, not
    // nested, so both are pinned to the default 22 to keep them on one ring
    // instead of the same-vertex stepping. None is a right angle ("two right
    // angles" is a sum). Markers are hidden in the static figure.
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","C","D","E","ABC","CDE","ABCt","CDEt",
                 "AB","BC","AC","CE","CD","DE"];
    var ANG1  = GIVEN.concat(["angABC","angDCE"]);
    var ANG2  = ANG1.concat(["angBAC","angCDE"]);
    var ANG   = ANG2.concat(["angACB","angCED"]);
    var MEET  = ANG.concat(["AF","DF","F"]);
    var PAR   = MEET.concat(["FB","FE"]);
    var PG    = PAR.concat(["FACD"]);
    var TRI   = PG.concat(["FBE"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "VI.4",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;170,130",
            "B;point;free;40,260",
            "C;point;free;200,260",
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;black;random",
            "E;point;lineSlider;B,C,290,260",
            "D1;point;parallelogram;A,B,C;0;0",
            "D2;point;parallelogram;A,C,E;0;0",
            "D;point;intersection;C,D1,E,D2",
            "CDE;polygon;triangle;C,D,E;0;0;black;random",
            "F;point;intersection;B,A,E,D",
            "AF;line;connect;A,F",
            "DF;line;connect;D,F",
            // Highlight targets, all transparent.
            "AB;line;connect;A,B;0;0;0",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C;0;0;0",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C;0;0;0",
            "CE;line;connect;C,E;0;0;0",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D;0;0;0",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E;0;0;0",
            "FB;line;connect;F,B;0;0;0",
            "FE;line;connect;F,E;0;0;0",
            "ABCt;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;0;0",
            "CDEt;polygon;triangle;C,D,E;0;0;0;0",
            "FACD;polygon;quadrilateral;F,A,C,D;0;0;0;0",
            "FBE;polygon;triangle;F,B,E;0;0;0;0",
            "angABC;sector;angleMarker;B,A,C;0;0;3b6ea5;9ec5e8",
            "angDCE;sector;angleMarker;C,D,E,22;0;0;3b6ea5;9ec5e8",
            "angBAC;sector;angleMarker;A,B,C;0;0;7a4fa3;cdb8e6",
            "angCDE;sector;angleMarker;D,C,E;0;0;7a4fa3;cdb8e6",
            "angACB;sector;angleMarker;C,A,B,22;0;0;3f8e4f;a8dbb0",
            "angCED;sector;angleMarker;E,C,D;0;0;3f8e4f;a8dbb0"
        ],
        // Draggables (checked against the bundle): A, B, C free and E a
        // lineSlider on BC — the two given triangles, standing from slide 1.
        // D and F are derived (intersection) and listed per slide; nothing
        // to defer.
        aliases: { "BA": "AB", "CB": "BC", "CA": "AC", "EC": "CE", "DC": "CD",
                   "ED": "DE", "BF": "FB", "EF": "FE", "FA": "AF", "FD": "DF",
                   "CBA": "angABC",
                   "DCE": "angDCE", "ECD": "angDCE",
                   "BAC": "angBAC", "CAB": "angBAC",
                   "EDC": "angCDE",
                   "ACB": "angACB", "BCA": "angACB",
                   "CED": "angCED", "DEC": "angCED" },
        slides: [
            { text: "“In equiangular triangles the sides about the equal angles are proportional where the corresponding sides are opposite the equal angles.”",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["ABCt","CDEt"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "VI.4" }] },
            { text: "Let {ABC|ABCt} and {DCE|CDEt} be equiangular triangles",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["ABCt","CDEt"] },
            { text: "having the angle {ABC|angABC} equal to the angle {DCE},",
              visible: ANG1,
              highlighted: ["angABC","angDCE"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angABC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angDCE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "the angle {BAC} equal to the angle {CDE|angCDE},",
              visible: ANG2,
              highlighted: ["angBAC","angCDE"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angBAC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angCDE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "and the angle {ACB} equal to the angle {CED}.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["angACB","angCED"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angACB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angCED", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "I say that in the triangles {ABC|ABCt} and {DEC|CDEt} the sides about the equal angles are proportional",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["ABCt","CDEt"] },
            { text: "where the corresponding sides are opposite the equal angles.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["angABC","angDCE","angBAC","angCDE","angACB","angCED"] },
            { text: "Let {BC} be placed in a straight line with {CE}.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["BC","CE"] },
            { text: "Then, since the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC} and {ACB} is less than two right angles,",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["angABC","angACB"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "two angles of a triangle are less than two right angles", ref: "I.17" }] },
            { text: "and the angle {ACB} equals the angle {DEC},",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["angACB","angCED"] },
            { text: "therefore the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC} and {DEC} is less than two right angles.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["angABC","angCED"] },
            { text: "Therefore {BA} and {ED}, when produced, will meet.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["AB","DE"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "lines making angles less than two right angles meet", ref: "I.Post.5" }] },
            { text: "Let them be produced and meet at {F}.",
              visible: MEET,
              highlighted: ["AF","DF","F"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "AF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeExtend },
                  { elem: "DF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeExtend },
                  { elem: "F",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] } },
            { text: "Now, since the angle {DCE} equals the angle {ABC|angABC}, {DC} is parallel to {FB}.",
              visible: PAR,
              highlighted: ["angDCE","angABC","CD","FB"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "DC parallel to FB", ref: "I.28" }] },
            { text: "Again, since the angle {ACB} equals the angle {DEC}, {AC} is parallel to {FE}.",
              visible: PAR,
              highlighted: ["angACB","angCED","AC","FE"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "AC parallel to FE", ref: "I.28" }] },
            { text: "Therefore {FACD} is a parallelogram,",
              visible: PG,
              highlighted: ["FACD"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "FACD", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline } ] } },
            { text: "therefore {FA} equals {DC}, and {AC} equals {FD}.",
              visible: PG,
              highlighted: ["AF","CD","AC","DF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "opposite sides of a parallelogram", ref: "I.34" }] },
            { text: "And, since {AC} is parallel to a side {FE} of the triangle {FBE},",
              visible: TRI,
              highlighted: ["AC","FE","FBE"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "FBE", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline } ] } },
            { text: "therefore {BA} is to {AF} as {BC} is to {CE}.",
              visible: TRI,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF","BC","CE"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "BA : AF = BC : CE", ref: "VI.2" }] },
            { text: "But {FD} equals {AC},",
              visible: TRI,
              highlighted: ["DF","AC"] },
            { text: "therefore {BC} is to {CE} as {AC} is to {DE},",
              visible: TRI,
              highlighted: ["BC","CE","AC","DE"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "equals have the same ratio", ref: "V.7" }] },
            { text: "and alternately {BC} is to {CA} as {CE} is to {ED}.",
              visible: TRI,
              highlighted: ["BC","AC","CE","DE"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "alternately", ref: "V.16" }] },
            { text: "Since then it was proved that {AB} is to {BC} as {DC} is to {CE},",
              visible: TRI,
              highlighted: ["AB","BC","CD","CE"] },
            { text: "and {BC} is to {CA} as {CE} is to {ED},",
              visible: TRI,
              highlighted: ["BC","AC","CE","DE"] },
            { text: "therefore, ex aequali, {BA} is to {AC} as {CD} is to {DE}.",
              visible: TRI,
              highlighted: ["AB","AC","CD","DE"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "ex aequali", ref: "V.22" }] },
            { text: "Therefore, in equiangular triangles the sides about the equal angles are proportional",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["ABCt","CDEt"] },
            { text: "where the corresponding sides are opposite the equal angles.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["angABC","angDCE","angBAC","angCDE","angACB","angCED"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "VI.4") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.5": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post5/",
        "I.17":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI17/",
        "I.28":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI28/",
        "I.34":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI34/",
        "V.7":      "/elements/books/bookV/propositions/propV7/",
        "V.16":     "/elements/books/bookV/propositions/propV16/",
        "V.22":     "/elements/books/bookV/propositions/propV22/",
        "VI.2":     "/elements/books/bookVI/propositions/propVI2/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Let {BC} be placed in a straight line with {CE}.

Then, since the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC} and {ACB} is less than two right angles, and the angle {ACB} equals the angle {DEC}, therefore the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC} and {DEC} is less than two right angles. Therefore {BA} and {ED}, when produced, will meet. Let them be produced and meet at {F}. [!just I.17; I.Post.5]

Now, since the angle {DCE} equals the angle {ABC|angABC}, {DC} is parallel to {FB}. Again, since the angle {ACB} equals the angle {DEC}, {AC} is parallel to {FE}. [!just I.28]

Therefore {FACD} is a parallelogram, therefore {FA} equals {DC}, and {AC} equals {FD}. [!just I.34]

And, since {AC} is parallel to a side {FE} of the triangle {FBE}, therefore {BA} is to {AF} as {BC} is to {CE}. [!just VI.2]

But {FD} equals {AC}, therefore {BC} is to {CE} as {AC} is to {DE}, and alternately {BC} is to {CA} as {CE} is to {ED}. [!just V.7; V.16]

Since then it was proved that {AB} is to {BC} as {DC} is to {CE}, and {BC} is to {CA} as {CE} is to {ED}, therefore, *ex aequali,* {BA} is to {AC} as {CD} is to {DE}. [!just V.22]

Therefore, *in equiangular triangles the sides about the equal angles are proportional where the corresponding sides are opposite the equal angles.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

In the enunciation of this proposition the term "equiangular triangles" refers to two triangles whose corresponding angles are equal, not to two triangles each of which is equiangular (equilateral).

Euclid has placed the triangles in particular positions in order to employ this particular proof. Such positioning is common in Book VI and is easily justified.

This proposition implies that equiangular triangles are similar, a fact proved in detail in the proof of proposition @VI.8. It also implies that triangles similar to the same triangle are similar to each other, also proved in detail in VI.8. The latter statement is generalized in @VI.21 to rectilinear figures in general.

This proposition is frequently used in the rest of Book VI starting with the next proposition, its converse. It is also used in Books X through XIII.
